%U A400728 3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3
%N A400728 The sequence of side lengths that occur when drawing a sequence of squares in the plane with integer side lengths such that the bottom-left corner of the k-th square is at the nonnegative integer point (k, y_k) and the square has side length a(k), subject to the conditions that (1) every initial segment has the property that the interior of each square has a distinct pattern made by the perimeters of the other squares, and (2) we greedily minimize the side length and then minimize the y-value.
%H A400728 Peter Kagey, <a href="https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/q/291882/53884">The Eternal Internal Patterns of Squares</a>, Code Golf Stack Exchange.
%e A400728 The first square has side length a(0)=1, and y-value A400729(0)=0; the next has side length a(1)=2 and y-value A400729(1)=0, and so on. The index of each square is marked in the lower right corner.
